        "math:real-exponent-laws",
        "cnm_h042"
      ],
      "explanation": "The numbers \\(10\\), \\(100\\), and \\(1000\\) are powers of the same base, so rewriting them makes their exponents comparable. Since \\(100=10^2\\) and \\(1000=10^3\\), the equation becomes\n\\[\n10^x(10^2)^{2x}=(10^3)^5,\n\\]\nand hence\n\\[\n10^{x+4x}=10^{15},\\qquad 10^{5x}=10^{15}.\n\\]\nAll bases are positive, so the power laws apply for every real \\(x\\). Powers of \\(10\\) strictly increase with their exponents, so equal powers have equal exponents. Therefore \\(5x=15\\), giving the unique solution \\(x=3\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).",
      "id": "common-base",
